# ============================================================================
# DISABLED 2026-09-06 — moved out of .github/workflows/ so GitHub never parses
# it. It does not run, and it cannot email. Nothing here is scheduled.
#
# WHY, and it is not fixable by editing this file:
#
#   A PUBLIC repository can only call reusable workflows from PUBLIC
#   repositories. jpurnell/BusinessMath is public; jpurnell/quality-gate-swift
#   is private. That is a GitHub access rule, not a permissions mistake.
#
# The symptom was a 0-second run with ZERO jobs, titled by file path rather
# than by `name:`, reported as "This run likely failed because of a workflow
# file issue". It failed on every push and on the 6am UTC schedule — ten
# failures in the two days before it was switched off — and it had been doing
# so since long before, on commits 2cdfc702, 9b1bcb61, 966502b6 and 2218a474.
#
# Ruled out, so do not re-investigate these:
#   - Secrets exist. CORPUS_DEPLOY_KEY and SPM_PRIVATE_DEPS_TOKEN were both set
#     on 2026-09-05, well before the runs that failed.
#   - The `permissions: contents: read / issues: write` block below is already
#     present. The comment beneath it diagnosing a missing permission as the
#     cause of "workflow file issue" is WRONG for this repo — that failure mode
#     is real, but it is not the one happening here.
#   - `gh api repos/jpurnell/quality-gate-swift/actions/permissions/access`
#     returns {"access_level":"user"}. That setting governs private-to-private
#     sharing and cannot grant a public caller access to a private callee.
#   - The callee file exists at the referenced path on `main`.
#
# TO RE-ENABLE, pick one and then move this file back to .github/workflows/:
#   1. Make jpurnell/quality-gate-swift public.
#   2. Vendor the gate's steps into this repo instead of calling it by `uses:`.
#   3. Make BusinessMath private — see the runner-economics warning below
#      before doing this, it is the expensive option.
#
# WHAT STILL ENFORCES THE GATE: the local pre-commit and pre-push hooks, which
# run all 45 checkers and block on failure. On a solo repo those were always
# the primary gate — the note below says as much. Nothing is unguarded; the
# gate simply is not duplicated in CI.
# ============================================================================

# Quality Gate — daily audit + push-triggered checks
#
# Prerequisites:
#   1. Repository secret CORPUS_DEPLOY_KEY — the SSH private key whose public
#      half is registered as a write-enabled deploy key on
#      jpurnell/org-judgement-corpus. Set it with:
#          gh secret set CORPUS_DEPLOY_KEY --repo jpurnell/<repo> < ~/.ssh/qg-corpus-deploy
#   2. A .quality-gate.yml in your project root
#   3. Repository secret holding a read-scoped PAT, passed as `dependency-token`.
#      This is NOT optional for most repos, and it is the requirement most
#      easily missed: the reusable workflow builds the gate by cloning
#      jpurnell/quality-gate-swift over HTTPS, and THAT REPO IS PRIVATE. Without
#      a token granting it read access, the build step fails for every consumer.
#      Grant the same token read on any private SPM dependencies the project
#      resolves. It needs no write anywhere, and no corpus access at all.
#
# BEFORE ENABLING, read the runner economics in Phase2_CIParity.md §3b.
# The reusable workflow runs on `macos-latest`. GitHub bills hosted macOS at a
# 10x multiplier on PRIVATE repos — roughly 200 macOS-minutes a month on the
# free tier, which this portfolio has exhausted in a single day's first pass.
# The June 2026 fleet-wide shutdown was that bill arriving. So:
#
#   Public repo   -> hosted macos-latest is free. Enable freely.
#   Private repo  -> register a self-hosted runner FIRST (§3c), or leave this
#                    workflow disabled and rely on local hooks, which are the
#                    primary gate on solo repos anyway (§3b).
#
# Enrollment is per-repo opt-in, not a blanket rollout: switch CI on when a
# repo gains a second contributor, which is when the §4b tripwire fires.

name: Quality Gate

on:
  push:
    branches: [main]
  schedule:
    - cron: '0 6 * * *'  # daily at 6am UTC
  # On demand — without this the gate can only be observed by pushing, which
  # makes "is CI actually running the gate?" a question you cannot ask.
  workflow_dispatch:

# A caller must grant at least what the callee declares, and the reusable gate
# declares `issues: write` so it can file a failure alert. Granting only
# `contents: read` here makes GitHub reject the file at parse time — a 0-second
# run titled by path rather than by name. That failure reports as "workflow file
# issue" and points at THIS file, while the missing permission is named in the
# callee, which is why it went undiagnosed across the fleet for months.
permissions:
  contents: read
  issues: write

jobs:
  quality-gate:
    uses: jpurnell/quality-gate-swift/.github/workflows/quality-gate-reusable.yml@main
    with:
      checks: "all"
      continue-on-failure: true
      corpus-repo: jpurnell/org-judgement-corpus
    secrets:
      corpus-deploy-key: ${{ secrets.CORPUS_DEPLOY_KEY }}
      # Required while quality-gate-swift is private — see prerequisite 3.
      dependency-token: ${{ secrets.SPM_PRIVATE_DEPS_TOKEN }}
